On Jul 5, 5:39 am, Janis <janis_papanag...@hotmail.com> wrote:
> The non-reliability in critical situations had been a problem more
> than once in my games. (It has been often mentioned that 0% failure
> in cure sickness is a lot better; just to mention one of the most
> critical.)

I concede the point on Cure Sickness, which is fatal if not cured very
quickly.  The other states, however, practically vanish from the game
once a unicorn horn is found.  Potions of blindness, hands down the
worst potion to drink in Rogue, are at worst a minor problem when you
have even an uncursed horn.

> The most powerful property is indeed restoring of all stats. I cannot
> quite see why Str, Dex, Con, Int, Wis, Cha should be restored. Plus
> hallucination, stunning, confusion, blindness, etc.; there are also
> other means to cure the latter (like sickness, lizard, carrot, etc.)
> and there's a potion and a spell for the former.

I forgot to mention this, but yes.  Considering how many sources of
stat drain there are, this seems very odd.  A unicorn horn will even
fix loss from stat abuse!


> > AND it well-enough identifies multiple potion types
>
> At the point when you meet (and can kill) unicorns identification of
> those few potions is not that important any more. And you don't even
> need to identify the potions by dipping; it's easier to quaff them and
> immediately use the horn or other means to cure you, if identification
> matters.

Well this is something I consider a bigger problem with Nethack, that
the item identification game has become too easy over time.

> > AND is a source of water for blessing
>
> Diluting potions in fountains and pools or moats is much easier at
> that point in the game and it is not restricted to the few potions
> which can be cleared by the horn (even the wand of cancellation is
> superior). Furthermore you might not want to dilute confusion; these
> potions are too helpful.

Diluting in fountains at least has the drawback that bad things can
happen, including stuff getting cursed.  Diluting in pools and moats
either requires a means of standing over the water, or that the player
strip and drop/store in an oilskin sack what he doesn't want damaged.

No reason the player needs to be confused, that come to mind at least,
is something that has to be done on a turn's notice, so casting a
forgotten spell works well enough.


> > AND is a well-above-average weapon
>
> Depending on whether you can use it at all. Off-top my head I cannot
> remember any character that wouldn't have had a better choice in his
> games than using a unicorn horn. And it's two-handed. And you need to
> increase your skill and waste slots.

All characters can use all weapons (except for some cross-aligned
artifacts).  It's true that many classes are restricted in unicorn
horn, but if something better has yet to be found, it can still be a
suitable stop-gap weapon.


> You need sufficient speed (maybe displacement or invisibility) and be
> not too wimpy to be able to corner it before you kill it. And that the
> horn is a guaranteed "death drop" is just consistent.

It is recognized that killing a unicorn is a big problem in the early
game, but then, I'm not talking about the early game, but more the mid
and late game.  The early game does not need to be made more
difficult, but mid and late could stand to be made harder.  And it's
being a consistent death drop is part of why it should be nerfed.


> I think the time when unicorns appear is well designed and the danger
> involved and difficulty in getting the horn (surely not equally
> difficult for all classes and all game constellations) well balanced.

That is pretty much correct.  And I think such a horn should be
immediately useful.  I just think, in the long term, a single unicorn
makes too many other things in the game much less interesting.

A game in which there is no obvious best strategy, generally, has a
stronger design than one in which there is an obvious best.  All
players who aren't doing a conduct, and who know what they're doing,
will win with a unicorn horn in inventory.  The existence of
Pestilence and Demogorgon demand that the player carry one at the very
least as a fallback measure.


> [...] But for most
> other properties there are other (often much better) solutions.

Take them all individually and you are right.  But consider them all
together in one object.  for most things a unicorn horn is more than
good enough, and there are a lot of things it can do, and it never
degrades with use; make sure it stays blessed, and it's good for the
rest of the game.

I don't necessarily think a unicorn horn should be made more
specialized.  Most of the things it can do are okay, but there should
be some cost to using them, or they should be rarer (make them good
wish options again), or something of that nature.

